          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "Please read the manuscript in /papers/investigations-3/13_contradiction_analysis/04_boundary_winding/threshold_routes/09_expanding_contour_later_collision/, the brainstorm note /09_expanding_contour_later_collision/brainstorm/01_CAP_AT_INFINITY_20261020.md, and the research overview /research-overview/03_ANALYTICAL_PHASE_SURPLUS_PROGRAM_20261020.\n\nBefore beginning on the research plan, I also want to discuss another approach here. I was just learning more about the De Bruijn Newman dynamics, and learned more about the simple zeros hypothesis, that all zeros on the critical line for the zeta function are simple. I think the methods here might also be applicable there. The change would be that we would assume that there is a non-simple zero at T=0 exactly, and try to derive a contradiction from that. This is a separate but clearly related route, and the same machinery can apply. Can you do an initial analysis here and write a note in the brainstorm folder, with the appropriate chat-history. If something comes from this, we will probably want to amend the research overview or write a parallel research overview note for this track. \n"
            ]
          },
          "status": "finished_successfully",
          "end_turn": null,
